<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{.Title}}</title>
<meta name="description" content="loop is a deterministic agentic harness in a single Go binary: YAML pipelines that run exactly as written, and chat rooms of persona agents that decide when to speak.">
<link rel="icon" href="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 32 32'%3E%3Ccircle cx='16' cy='16' r='11' fill='none' stroke='%233fb950' stroke-width='5'/%3E%3C/svg%3E">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{.Root}}site.css">
</head>
<body>
<nav>
  <a class="brand" href="{{.Root}}index.html"><span class="brand-mark"></span>loop</a>
  <a href="{{.Root}}docs/"{{if eq .Active "docs"}} class="on"{{end}}>docs</a>
  <span class="spacer"></span>
  <a class="gh" href="https://github.com/shafi-/loop">github</a>
</nav>

<div class="landing">
  <header class="hero">
    <h1>Determinism where work runs.<br><span class="accent">Judgment where models speak.</span></h1>
    <p class="sub">loop is a deterministic agentic harness in a single Go binary.
    Describe work as YAML pipelines that run <em>exactly</em> as written, and talk to
    teams of persona agents in chat rooms that command those pipelines —
    with you holding every approval.</p>
    <div class="install">
      <code id="install-cmd">curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/shafi-/loop/main/install.sh | sh</code>
      <button id="copy-install" type="button" aria-label="copy install command">copy</button>
    </div>
    <p class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn primary" href="{{.Root}}docs/">read the docs</a>
      <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/shafi-/loop">source &amp; stars</a>
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="terminals">
    <div class="term">
      <div class="term-bar"><span></span><span></span><span></span><em>a pipeline run</em></div>
      <pre>$ loop run feature-delivery.yaml
▶ pipeline feature-delivery
→ requirements (llm)
→ architect (agent)
  🔧 architect wrote plans/drone.md
→ approval (human)
── your input needed ──
  Ship this plan? yes / no / describe changes
✓ run complete (4 steps) — resume id kept</pre>
    </div>
    <div class="term">
      <div class="term-bar"><span></span><span></span><span></span><em>a room, in the browser</em></div>
      <pre class="chat-mock"><span class="u">You</span> — we need a mitigation plan for the EU payment timeouts
<span class="a c1">@commander</span> On it — starting the respond pipeline now.
<span class="evt">You started respond pipeline</span>
<span class="evt live">respond · → approve (human)</span>
<span class="a c2">@sre</span> Blast radius is unknown; I want post-fix numbers
      before anyone says "resolved".
<span class="a c3">@comms</span> Holding language ready — nothing public until
      verification lands.
<span class="gate">✋ respond needs your approval — [yes] [no]</span></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="features">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Pipelines</h3>
      <p>Ordered stages — <code>llm</code>, <code>agent</code>, <code>tool</code>,
      <code>human</code>, <code>router</code> — with deterministic flow, a full
      audit trail, and resume-after-failure. Gates understand
      <strong>yes / no / your words</strong>, so free-form answers route
      deterministically.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Rooms</h3>
      <p>Teams of persona agents. Tag <code>@name</code> and it must reply;
      untagged agents read along and judge for themselves whether to speak.
      Rooms own pipelines: run them in-chat, several concurrently, and approve
      gates without leaving the conversation.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>One engine, three doors</h3>
      <p><code>loop serve</code> owns every run — runs outlive your terminal, and
      a gate asked anywhere is answerable everywhere: the browser dashboard, the
      attached terminal, or a hosted room. Or skip the daemon entirely;
      plain <code>loop run</code> stays zero-setup.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="quickstart">
    <h2>Two minutes to first run</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p><strong>Install and onboard</strong> — the onboarding command checks your
        provider config and installs the agent executor (no Node.js needed afterward):</p>
        <pre>loop setup</pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><strong>Set one key</strong> — provider, model, and endpoint all resolve from it:</p>
        <pre>export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-...   # or OPENAI_API_KEY</pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><strong>Scaffold and run</strong>:</p>
        <pre>loop init
loop run pipelines/feature-delivery.yaml</pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><strong>Open a room</strong> — or run the whole thing as a service:</p>
        <pre>loop chat rooms/leadership.yaml "We need a new drone shoot-out"
# or: loop serve  +  loop ui   →  http://127.0.0.1:8787</pre>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="examples">
    <h2>See it worked out end to end</h2>
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="https://github.com/shafi-/loop/tree/main/examples/agency-intake">agency-intake →</a></h3>
      <p>A client request enters a chat room, an intake team interrogates it, and
      the room's settled transcript becomes the input of a delivery pipeline —
      judgment first, determinism after.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="https://github.com/shafi-/loop/tree/main/examples/incident-room">incident-room →</a></h3>
      <p>An incident response team — commander, SRE, comms — debates around a gated
      mitigation pipeline. The SRE reads the last run's artifacts before trusting
      the fix. Five minutes, one key.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>

